### What is level order traversal?

Level order traversal visits every node in a binary tree from top to bottom, processing all nodes at the same depth before moving deeper. Within each level, nodes are visited left to right. The result is a list of lists, where each inner list contains the values of one level.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Queue;

public class LevelOrderTraversal {
    static class TreeNode {
        int val;
        TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int v) { val = v; }
    }

    public static List<List<Integer>> levelOrder(TreeNode root) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) return result;

        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer(root);

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int levelSize = queue.size();
            List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
                TreeNode node = queue.poll();
                level.add(node.val);
                if (node.left != null) queue.offer(node.left);
                if (node.right != null) queue.offer(node.right);
            }
            result.add(level);
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Build tree:     1
        //               /   \
        //              2     3
        //             / \     \
        //            4   5     6
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(1);
        root.left = new TreeNode(2);
        root.right = new TreeNode(3);
        root.left.left = new TreeNode(4);
        root.left.right = new TreeNode(5);
        root.right.right = new TreeNode(6);

        System.out.println(levelOrder(root));
    }
}
```

```text
[[1], [2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]
```

### Why does BFS use a queue?

Breadth-first search visits nodes in the order they were discovered, which is exactly FIFO behavior. The root is discovered first, then its children, then their children. A queue processes them in that discovery order. Using a stack instead would give depth-first order.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Queue;

public class WhyQueue {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Queue<String> bfs = new ArrayDeque<>();
        bfs.offer("Root: Branch Manager");
        bfs.offer("Level 1: Loan Officer");
        bfs.offer("Level 1: Teller Lead");

        System.out.println("BFS order (FIFO):");
        while (!bfs.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("  " + bfs.poll());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
BFS order (FIFO):
  Root: Branch Manager
  Level 1: Loan Officer
  Level 1: Teller Lead
```

### How does the size snapshot separate levels?

At the start of each outer iteration, `queue.size()` tells you exactly how many nodes belong to the current level. You process that many nodes and no more. While processing, you enqueue children, which belong to the next level. Because you only dequeue the snapshot count, the children wait in the queue for the next iteration.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Queue;

public class SizeSnapshot {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Queue<String> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer("A"); // level 0

        int level = 0;
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int size = queue.size(); // snapshot
            System.out.println("Level " + level + " has " + size + " nodes");
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                String node = queue.poll();
                System.out.println("  Processing: " + node);
                // Simulate adding children
                if (node.equals("A")) { queue.offer("B"); queue.offer("C"); }
                if (node.equals("B")) { queue.offer("D"); }
            }
            level++;
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Level 0 has 1 nodes
  Processing: A
Level 1 has 2 nodes
  Processing: B
  Processing: C
Level 2 has 1 nodes
  Processing: D
```

### What are the edge cases?

An empty tree (null root) returns an empty list. A single-node tree returns one level with one element. A left-skewed or right-skewed tree returns one node per level. The algorithm handles all of these because the size snapshot adapts to however many nodes exist at each depth.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LevelOrderEdgeCases {
    static class TreeNode {
        int val; TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int v) { val = v; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Null root
        System.out.println("Null: " + LevelOrderTraversal.levelOrder(null));

        // Single node
        System.out.println("Single: " + LevelOrderTraversal.levelOrder(new LevelOrderTraversal.TreeNode(42)));

        // Skewed
        LevelOrderTraversal.TreeNode skewed = new LevelOrderTraversal.TreeNode(1);
        skewed.left = new LevelOrderTraversal.TreeNode(2);
        skewed.left.left = new LevelOrderTraversal.TreeNode(3);
        System.out.println("Skewed: " + LevelOrderTraversal.levelOrder(skewed));
    }
}
```

```text
Null: []
Single: [[42]]
Skewed: [[1], [2], [3]]
```

### What are the common BFS variants?

Zigzag level order alternates left-to-right and right-to-left between levels. Right-side view collects only the last node of each level. Per-level average computes the mean value per level. All of these reuse the same size-snapshot queue loop and differ only in what they collect from each level.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Queue;
import java.util.Collections;

public class BfsVariants {
    static class TreeNode {
        int val; TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int v) { val = v; }
    }

    // Zigzag: reverse odd-numbered levels
    public static List<List<Integer>> zigzag(TreeNode root) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) return result;
        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer(root);
        boolean leftToRight = true;

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int size = queue.size();
            List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                TreeNode n = queue.poll();
                level.add(n.val);
                if (n.left != null) queue.offer(n.left);
                if (n.right != null) queue.offer(n.right);
            }
            if (!leftToRight) Collections.reverse(level);
            result.add(level);
            leftToRight = !leftToRight;
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(1);
        root.left = new TreeNode(2);
        root.right = new TreeNode(3);
        root.left.left = new TreeNode(4);
        root.left.right = new TreeNode(5);
        root.right.right = new TreeNode(6);
        System.out.println("Zigzag: " + zigzag(root));
    }
}
```

```text
Zigzag: [[1], [3, 2], [4, 5, 6]]
```

### What about time and space complexity?

Time is O(n) because every node is enqueued and dequeued exactly once. Space is O(w) where w is the maximum width of the tree, because the queue at its fullest holds one entire level. For a balanced binary tree, the widest level has roughly n/2 nodes.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class BfsComplexity {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Time: O(n) - each node enqueued and dequeued once");
        System.out.println("Space: O(w) - queue holds one full level");
        System.out.println("Balanced tree: widest level has ~n/2 nodes");
        System.out.println("Skewed tree: widest level has 1 node -> O(1) space");
    }
}
```

```text
Time: O(n) - each node enqueued and dequeued once
Space: O(w) - queue holds one full level
Balanced tree: widest level has ~n/2 nodes
Skewed tree: widest level has 1 node -> O(1) space
```

### How is this applied in banking?

At the National Bank of India, I used level order traversal to produce a rank-by-rank view of the loan approval hierarchy for the compliance dashboard. Reviewers see all approvers at the same authority level grouped together. The left-to-right ordering within each rank encodes seniority, and the queue-based traversal with a fixed count per level keeps both grouping and ordering correct.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Queue;

public class ApprovalHierarchy {
    static class Approver {
        String name;
        List<Approver> reports = new ArrayList<>();
        Approver(String n) { name = n; }
    }

    public static void printByRank(Approver root) {
        Queue<Approver> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer(root);
        int rank = 0;
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int size = queue.size();
            System.out.print("Rank " + rank + ": ");
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                Approver a = queue.poll();
                System.out.print(a.name + " ");
                for (Approver r : a.reports) queue.offer(r);
            }
            System.out.println();
            rank++;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Approver gm = new Approver("Pankaj(GM)");
        Approver mgr1 = new Approver("Rajesh(Mgr)");
        Approver mgr2 = new Approver("Priya(Mgr)");
        gm.reports.add(mgr1);
        gm.reports.add(mgr2);
        mgr1.reports.add(new Approver("Amit(Officer)"));
        mgr2.reports.add(new Approver("Neha(Officer)"));
        printByRank(gm);
    }
}
```

```text
Rank 0: Pankaj(GM) 
Rank 1: Rajesh(Mgr) Priya(Mgr) 
Rank 2: Amit(Officer) Neha(Officer) 
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The size-snapshot technique is the single most important BFS pattern to internalize. Almost every level-aware tree problem, from zigzag to right-side view to level sums, reuses the exact same outer loop with only the inner collection logic changed. Recognizing this pattern lets you solve an entire family of problems from a single template.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SeniorBfsNotes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Template: while (!queue.isEmpty()) { int size = queue.size(); ... }");
        System.out.println("Variant: zigzag -> reverse alternate levels");
        System.out.println("Variant: right-side view -> take last node of each level");
        System.out.println("Variant: level averages -> sum and divide per level");
        System.out.println("All share the same size-snapshot outer loop");
    }
}
```

```text
Template: while (!queue.isEmpty()) { int size = queue.size(); ... }
Variant: zigzag -> reverse alternate levels
Variant: right-side view -> take last node of each level
Variant: level averages -> sum and divide per level
All share the same size-snapshot outer loop
```
